riot games and its ceo are being sued  for workplace harassment  and gender discrimination the recently  filed complaint caps off a years-long  investigation  into an allegedly toxic workplace the  gaming giant has been sued for both of  these things before  as well as allegedly firing women who've  decided to speak out  riot says they've made a lot of changes  since the allegations were made  but this complaint begs to differ on  february 9th  a legal complaint was filed by former  riot employee sharon o'donnell  against the company and her former boss  ceo nicolo laurent  i say complaint because there is a  difference between a legal complaint and  a lawsuit  a complaint refers to all of the  documents filed explaining the  allegations  while a lawsuit refers to the actual  legal proceedings that take place in  court  o'donnell was hired as laurent's  assistant in october of 2017  and was eventually fired in july of  2020. the complaint alleges that  throughout her time working for riot  o'donnell experienced multiple instances  of harassment and gender discrimination  quote shortly after the plaintiff was  hired the defendant laurent  began a pattern of harassing the  plaintiff based on her sex  or gender this continued until the end  of her employment end quote the  complaint includes a laundry list of  specific allegations  one of which is an instance where  laurent allegedly asked o'donnell to  come over to his house  while his wife and kids were away note  the spelling  the complaint alleges that laurent  constantly demeaned o'donnell and  references a time where laurent was  allegedly  quote staring at her in a sexual fashion  when discussing his underwear  while telling the plaintiff that he  really was a size extra large  but that he just liked a tight fit end  quote  on top of that the complaint alleges  that o'donnell was forced to work  overtime she was never paid for  and rarely was given her legally  mandated work breaks  the complaint also states that once she  refused laurent's advances and went to  hr to complain about his behavior  her work duties were stripped away and  she was eventually fired the complaint  says that by not paying her for her  overtime work and not giving her her  legally manded work breaks  riot violated the fair employment and  housing act or fia  in the united states and if you didn't  know fia was passed in 1959  to help fight against sexual harassment  and other forms of  unlawful discrimination in employment  and housing the complaint also alleges  that riot games as a company  failed to take all of the necessary  steps to stop this alleged behavior  and essentially just ignored everyone  who came forward to talk about it  according to the complaint things got so  bad for o'donnell that she had to seek  out medical attention  quote as a further and proximate result  of the discrimination as a four pled  the plaintiff was required to and did  seek medical attention  and will need medical attention in the  future end quote now  o'donnell's experience that we've been  talking about so far is not an isolated  incident  in fact this story goes all the way back  to 2018.  in august of 2018 kotaku published an  in-depth investigation into the  workplace culture of  riot by speaking to 28 current and  former employees  the investigation was able to highlight  a series of systemic issues  relating to alleged sexism harassment  and abuse of power by management in  particular the piece describes a  workplace where women were consistently  turned down for leadership positions  as well as a list that was allegedly  passed around by senior management  describing women at the company they'd  like to sleep with by november of 2018  a few months after the original story  broke one current  at the time and one former riot employee  filed a class-action lawsuit against the  company  alleging systemic gender discrimination  developer surveys  consistently find that discrimination  and sexism  are endemic to the gaming industry but  understand how bad it's gotten  we have to look at one company in  particular that is  crushing it on the sexism leaderboard  riot games just can't escape  controversy this week the league of  legends developer was slammed with a  class-action lawsuit claiming that the  company fosters a sexist work  environment  in the ensuing aftermath of the  reporting and the class action lawsuit  riot went into full-on crisis mode they  rolled out a bunch of diversity  initiatives on their website  and basically said hey we're not  actually women hating douchebags  this continued into 2019 as riot doubled  down on the whole  or changing promise but the thing that  made this go international and caused  one of the biggest walkouts that the  games industry has ever seen  was something called forced arbitration  which is a tactic designed to stop  employees suing for all sorts of  workplace violations  including gender discrimination and  harassment it works like this if an  employee files a workplace lawsuit  against their employer  forced arbitration makes it so that  there's no public trial  instead a company hires a private  litigator to reach a settlement behind  closed doors  away from any public spotlight it's a  pretty nefarious tactic that companies  like google have stated that they've  stopped using  not riot games though because in april  of 2019 almost  eight months after that original kotaku  investigation  riot filed a motion blocking employees  from suing them for harassment  in other words forced arbitration  according to riot at the time the  argument was simple  as soon as these women were hired they  waived any right to sue  on may 6 2019 riot games employees and  other games industry employees from  around the world  staged a gigantic walkout in protest of  riot's forced arbitration policies  and the entire situation as a whole we  reported on this at the time  in one of the very early episodes of  this show  over the past few months riot games has  been under a ton of criticism  current and former employees have  accused the company of fostering a toxic  sexist workplace culture and breaking  california labor laws  which all led to a walkout last month  and based on how it went  we're in for a lot more action in  response to the walkouts  riot announced they'd be changing their  policy on forced arbitration  sort of quote ultimately given the  complexities of ongoing litigation  we will not change our employee  agreements while in active litigation  everything we've heard will impact our  discussions when we revisit arbitration  and we hope to have an answer that would  be satisfying to everyone  at a minimum we will give new riders the  option to opt out of arbitration on  individual sexual harassment claims  end quote so what riot essentially said  here was hey  if you're currently suing us because  we're shitty well  tough luck but in the future if you sue  us for being shitty well then we won't  force you into closed-door arbitration  on top of that wishy-washy statement  riot announced a new diversity and  inclusion department with the goal to  quote  leverage a group of engaged and  thoughtful writers from teams across the  company  to participate in formal discussions on  d and i  end quote all of this was happening  throughout the summer of 2019  and by december of that same year the  class action lawsuit that was filed in  2018  finally came to an end a court ordered  riot to pay out 10 million dollars  spread out amongst every woman who had  worked for riot in the past  five years and according to one of the  plaintiff's lawyers at the time  this settlement was one of the largest  in california history for gender  discrimination lawsuits  from there things died down for a while  and everyone hoped that riot was taking  their diversity initiative seriously  which brings us back to now  almost two years later on the cusp of  another lawsuit  alleging the same sexist behavior this  time  leveled at the ceo of the company and in  terms of a resolution  o'donnell is looking for compensation  over the lost wages from the  overtime she worked as well as damages  stemming from the emotional  and mental health turmoil she  experienced while working for riot and  laurent  now i reached out to riot for a  statement about this whole situation  and here's what they had to say quote in  this case  because some of the claims relate to an  executive leader  a special committee of our board of  directors is overseeing the  investigation  which is being conducted by an outside  law firm our ceo has pledged his full  cooperation and support during this  process  and we're committed to ensuring that all  claims are thoroughly explored  and appropriately resolved one subject  we can address  immediately is the plaintiff's claim  about their separation from riot  the plaintiff was dismissed from the  company over seven months ago  following more than a dozen complaints  from both employees  and external partners and after offering  multiple coaching discussions to try and  address these concerns  any suggestion otherwise is simply false  end quote so almost two years later have  things  actually changed at riot they would  argue yes  pointing to multiple diversity  initiatives that they've started  but it's a pretty rough look when the  ceo  of the very company that says it's  changing for the better  is being accused of the exact same  things that caused one of the biggest  walkouts in the history of the games  industry  thanks for watching if you want more  content like this  hit the sub button and ring that  notification bell for unique bite size  videos you won't find anywhere else  hit up our instagram twitter and  facebook pages  you
 

